  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 28f310e05cc019a48855581da30b411e, type: 3}
  m_Name: TamalesHistory
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lines:
  - text: Time for something wrapped in tradition Cavite Tamales! Before we prepare
      this regional delicacy.
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Let me tell you the history and origin of tamales.
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Cavite Tamales reflects the cultural exchanges that took place during.
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: "The Spanish colonial period, when the Philippines and Mexico were connected
      through the Manila\u2013Acapulco Galleon Trade."
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Unlike Mexican tamales, which are generally made with corn masa, the Cavite
      version commonly uses ground rice or rice flour.
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: ' Depending on the recipe, it may contain peanuts, chicken, chickpeas,
      and egg, all wrapped in banana leaves and steamed.'
    characterSprite: {fileID: -511706578061346315, guid: ae71825725b21a3459805439433622ee, type: 3}
  - text: Did you know?
    characterSprite: {fileID: -8056027616224820867, guid: 783feefcd5809a142b7b39ffbbe1c917, type: 3}
  - text: Filipino and Mexican food traditions influenced each other through centuries
      of contact, but Cavite developed its own version using local ingredients!
    characterSprite: {fileID: -8056027616224820867, guid: 783feefcd5809a142b7b39ffbbe1c917, type: 3}
  - text: Let's prepare those banana leaves and wrap everything carefully before
      steaming!
    characterSprite: {fileID: -8056027616224820867, guid: 783feefcd5809a142b7b39ffbbe1c917, type: 3}
